config BR2_PACKAGE_THINGINO_MCU_WDT
	bool "thingino-mcu-wdt"
	select BR2_PACKAGE_JSON_C
	help
	  Disables the hardware watchdog on a discrete companion MCU (found on
	  some Ingenic camera boards, e.g. the MindMotion MM32F0020B1T on the
	  Cinnado B6 T23ZN/TM18NA LTE variant) as early as possible during boot.

	  On boards where this MCU is present, its watchdog can power-cycle the
	  whole SoC if not disabled quickly after boot. This package ships two
	  of the vendor's own extracted binaries (mcu_test, a diagnostic tool,
	  plus its libcommon.so dependency) and runs `mcu_test --wdt_disable`
	  from the earliest available init hook (an F-prefixed script in
	  /etc/init.d, run by rcF before rcS), rather than reimplementing the
	  I2C wire protocol from scratch.

	  Only enable this for boards that actually have this MCU wired up.

	  NOTE: an earlier version of this package shipped a different vendor
	  binary, disable_mcu_wdt (extracted from a separate recovery-initramfs
	  tool, statically linked against only libc/libpthread). It reliably
	  failed with garbled I2C responses on live hardware -- the I2C bus,
	  pins, and timing were all independently confirmed correct (a plain
	  MCU version query and other mcu_test commands succeed cleanly on the
	  same bus), so the bug was specific to that one binary's own protocol
	  handling, not the transport. mcu_test --wdt_disable, using the
	  identical I2C bus/MCU, succeeds cleanly every time. Use mcu_test.

	  mcu_test needs libjson-c.so.5; Thingino's own json-c package builds
	  libjson-c.so (unversioned), so the init script symlinks it at runtime
	  rather than requiring a second copy of the library.
